<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babullah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1570">1570</a>–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Babullah of Ternate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babullah was sultan of Ternate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> from 1570 to 1583. After the Portuguese murdered his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hairun">Hairun</a>, he besieged their fort on Ternate until they surrendered it in 1575, and extended the sultanate's influence over many islands of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. In 1579 he received the English navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babullah.jpg" alt="Babullah" width="294" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HHEHUM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babullah_1579_by_De_Bry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Babullah, known as Prince Baab before his accession, was the oldest or one of the oldest sons of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hairun">Hairun</a> and his consort Boki Tanjung of Bacan. His father had him trained in the Qur'an and in warfare. He accompanied Hairun into temporary exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> in 1545 to 1546 and co-signed an act of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> in 1560, the oldest preserved Indonesian letter with seals. Portuguese sources name him heir to the throne.</p><p>Ternate, a centre of the clove trade, had depended on the Portuguese since 1522, when they built a stone fort there. Resentment grew over the conduct of their commanders. In 1563 Baab led a fleet against the Christian village of Nusaniwi on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a> and mounted an expedition to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, and he was displeased that his father was indulgent towards the Europeans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 25 February 1570 Captain Diogo Lopes de Mesquita invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hairun">Hairun</a> to the fort of São João Baptista, and Martim Afonso Pimentel stabbed him as he left. A royal council with the princes and sub-rulers met on the island of Hiri and made Baab sultan as Babullah Datu Syah. He proclaimed an irreconcilable enmity towards the Portuguese, which corresponded to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, and married a sister of Sultan Gapi Baguna of Tidore.</p><p>The Portuguese fortresses on Ternate other than São João Baptista fell quickly, and Ternatan forces besieged that fort and cut its supplies. A fleet also attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a>. After five years of siege Babullah gave the garrison an ultimatum to leave within 24 hours. On 15 July 1575 the Portuguese left, and no one was hurt. Ternatans born on the island could remain as royal subjects. Babullah converted the fort into his palace, renamed Gammalamo, and let Portuguese merchants trade on the same terms as others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 3 November 1579 Babullah received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a>, who described him as tall and of a princely countenance. The sultan declared friendship with Queen Elizabeth, apparently to set the English against the Portuguese, and sent a ring to her; Drake declined to join a campaign against the Portuguese fort on Tidore. In 1580 Babullah is said to have led a naval expedition through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, concluded an alliance with the king of Gowa and built a fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somba-opu">Somba Opu</a>.</p><p>He became known as ruler of 72 islands in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and sultans under him governed regions through deputy rulers called Sangaji, though outlying areas were only tributaries. He died in July 1583. He is remembered as the sultan who forced the Portuguese out of Ternate, an event described as delaying Western colonisation of the archipelago for the better part of a century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babullah of Ternate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1570 they murdered Sultan Hairun, and his son <b>Babullah</b> besieged the fort until the Portuguese left in 1575.</blockquote><a href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
